WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.760
Marcus operates a small business and considers a simplified employee pension,

2
00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:06.880
usually called a SEP.

3
00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:11.740
A regular SEP uses employer contributions to individual retirement

4
00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:14.220
arrangements established for eligible workers.

5
00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:17.920
The employer can decide whether to contribute for a year, but

6
00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:21.860
cannot ignore required employee coverage or the applicable allocation rules

7
00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:26.500
when making contributions. Contributions belong to the employee immediately;

8
00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:30.420
a SEP does not use a waiting period for ownership of

9
00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:35.400
those deposits. A regular SEP contribution is not an employee salary

10
00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:38.460
deferral under a new four-oh-one-k plan.

11
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:43.180
The SEP is the employer arrangement, while the individual account and

12
00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:45.760
its investments hold the contributed funds.

13
00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:49.780
Keep those layers distinct when identifying the product on an exam.

14
00:00:50.679 --> 00:00:54.099
A question may call a retirement plan for a self-employed person

15
00:00:54.099 --> 00:00:56.599
a Keogh or H R ten plan.

16
00:00:57.039 --> 00:01:00.319
These are older names associated with qualified retirement plans for

17
00:01:00.319 --> 00:01:03.279
self-employed individuals and unincorporated businesses.

18
00:01:03.959 --> 00:01:07.579
They do not identify one unique insurance policy or one guaranteed

19
00:01:07.579 --> 00:01:11.579
investment return. A self-employed business can use different qualified

20
00:01:11.579 --> 00:01:15.959
designs, including defined contribution or defined benefit arrangements.

21
00:01:15.959 --> 00:01:20.519
The plan still must satisfy its applicable eligibility, contribution

22
00:01:21.039 --> 00:01:22.739
and other qualification requirements.

23
00:01:23.499 --> 00:01:27.639
If the business has eligible common-law employees, the owner cannot assume

24
00:01:27.639 --> 00:01:29.599
those workers disappear from the analysis.

25
00:01:30.459 --> 00:01:34.099
First identify the business and the plan structure, then determine

26
00:01:34.099 --> 00:01:37.759
contributions and benefits under that structure rather than choosing an

27
00:01:37.759 --> 00:01:38.799
answer from the name alone.

28
00:01:39.506 --> 00:01:43.186
In a profit-sharing plan, the employer generally has discretion over how

29
00:01:43.186 --> 00:01:46.606
much to contribute, subject to the plan and legal requirements.

30
00:01:47.506 --> 00:01:49.946
The name does not mean the business must show a profit

31
00:01:49.946 --> 00:01:52.166
before any contribution is possible.

32
00:01:52.866 --> 00:01:57.486
A four-oh-one-k feature allows employees to elect salary deferrals, and the

33
00:01:57.486 --> 00:02:00.566
employer may also make matching or other permitted contributions.

34
00:02:01.606 --> 00:02:03.386
A plan can combine these features.

35
00:02:04.046 --> 00:02:07.986
Do not confuse employer discretion in a profit-sharing contribution with an

36
00:02:07.986 --> 00:02:12.726
employee's salary-reduction election. If a question describes Elena choosing

37
00:02:12.726 --> 00:02:16.166
to defer part of her wages, that fact points to the

38
00:02:16.166 --> 00:02:21.086
employee deferral feature. If it describes the employer deciding an annual

39
00:02:21.086 --> 00:02:25.446
contribution under a qualifying formula, identify the employer contribution

40
00:02:25.446 --> 00:02:30.782
being discussed. A SIMPLE individual retirement account plan is designed

41
00:02:30.782 --> 00:02:34.822
for eligible small employers, generally those with one hundred or fewer

42
00:02:34.822 --> 00:02:38.922
qualifying employees, and ordinarily no other retirement plan.

43
00:02:39.402 --> 00:02:43.802
Employees may make salary-reduction contributions, and the employer must make

44
00:02:43.802 --> 00:02:47.382
the required contribution under the selected permitted formula.

45
00:02:48.102 --> 00:02:52.122
Common standard formulas use a match up to three percent of

46
00:02:52.122 --> 00:02:56.342
compensation or a two percent nonelective contribution for eligible

47
00:02:56.342 --> 00:03:01.442
employees. Current rules allow additional variations for specified employers

48
00:03:01.442 --> 00:03:05.442
and plan elections, so those percentages are not a universal statement

49
00:03:05.442 --> 00:03:07.002
of every SIMPLE arrangement.

50
00:03:07.842 --> 00:03:10.422
All SIMPLE account contributions are immediately vested.

51
00:03:11.162 --> 00:03:14.922
Distinguish a required employer contribution from a promise that every worker

52
00:03:14.922 --> 00:03:17.022
will choose to defer part of their own wages.

53
00:03:17.801 --> 00:03:22.201
Rosa works for a public school offering a four-oh-three-b retirement plan.

54
00:03:22.981 --> 00:03:27.081
These arrangements also serve eligible tax-exempt organizations, including

55
00:03:27.081 --> 00:03:30.541
qualifying organizations under section five-oh-one-c-three.

56
00:03:31.401 --> 00:03:36.621
The term tax-sheltered annuity appears in study materials, but

57
00:03:36.621 --> 00:03:39.941
permitted funding is not limited to an insurance annuity contract.

58
00:03:40.861 --> 00:03:45.441
A qualifying custodial account invested in mutual funds can also be

59
00:03:45.441 --> 00:03:49.181
used, and church retirement income accounts have their own permitted

60
00:03:49.181 --> 00:03:54.341
structure. Employee salary reductions commonly fund the plan, and employer

61
00:03:54.341 --> 00:03:56.341
contributions may also be available.

62
00:03:57.201 --> 00:04:00.921
A regular private for-profit employer does not become an eligible

63
00:04:00.921 --> 00:04:05.141
four-oh-three-b sponsor merely by choosing an annuity investment.

64
00:04:05.781 --> 00:04:09.221
Identify the sponsoring organization before applying the plan label.

65
00:04:09.974 --> 00:04:11.094
Put the cases together.

66
00:04:11.674 --> 00:04:15.114
Marcus's employer-funded individual retirement arrangement points toward a

67
00:04:15.114 --> 00:04:19.434
SEP. Elena's elective salary deferrals point toward a four-oh-one-k feature.

68
00:04:20.054 --> 00:04:23.234
Rosa's eligible public-school plan points toward four-oh-three-b.

69
00:04:23.714 --> 00:04:27.114
A qualifying small employer combining employee salary reductions with

70
00:04:27.114 --> 00:04:29.354
required employer contributions may use SIMPLE.

71
00:04:29.914 --> 00:04:32.474
The same annuity investment could appear in more than one arrangement

72
00:04:32.474 --> 00:04:34.474
without making their legal rules identical.

73
00:04:35.274 --> 00:04:37.654
Also identify pretax and Roth treatment separately.

74
00:04:37.654 --> 00:04:41.654
Current law permits Roth options in specified SEP and SIMPLE arrangements;

75
00:04:41.714 --> 00:04:44.754
old claims that they can never offer Roth treatment are outdated.

76
00:04:45.514 --> 00:04:49.434
Before answering, identify the sponsor, contributor, ownership rule, plan

77
00:04:49.434 --> 00:04:52.054
requirements and tax character actually stated in the question.
